--TEST--
`Core\Uri::decodeComponent` at its two edges: a `%` that does not begin a well-formed escape decodes to itself, and a well-formed escape for an octet no `string` can hold is refused — a lone byte, a truncated sequence, a surrogate and an overlong form alike, which is where this member diverges from PHP's `rawurldecode` — spec § 12, ADR 0009
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The neighbouring cases show this member against its encoder, over every
// ASCII byte and against `decodeFormValue`. What none of them asks is where it
// stops: `decode` reads a `%` as an escape only when two hex digits follow it,
// and it answers *octets*, which a `string` then has to be able to hold. Both
// edges are named here with the value on each side of them.
//
// The first edge is textual and PHP agrees with us on it: an escape needs both
// digits, so a trailing `%`, a one-digit tail and a non-hex pair are each left
// exactly as written rather than repaired or refused. `%41` on the other side
// of that line is the same three characters read as `A`.
array<string> $textual = ["a%", "a%4", "a%zzb", "%41", "%2541"];
foreach ($textual as string $row) {
    echo "[", $row, "] -> [", Core\Uri::decodeComponent($row), "]\n";
}

// The second edge is the one ADR 0009 draws, and it is a real divergence from
// PHP: a PHP string is a byte string, so `rawurldecode("a%FFb")` there answers
// three bytes that are not text; a Novis `string` is UTF-8, so there is no
// value for this member to answer and it throws instead. Every family of
// invalid sequence is refused, not only the obviously-out-of-range byte —
// counted over the table, because a member validating the first octet alone
// still accepts a truncated sequence and a surrogate.
array<string> $octets = [
    "a%FFb",
    "%C3",
    "%ED%A0%80",
    "%C0%80",
    "%F5%80%80%80",
];
int $tried = 0;
int $refused = 0;
foreach ($octets as string $bad) {
    $tried = $tried + 1;
    try {
        // The call is bound before the `echo`, because `echo` writes its
        // earlier operands before a later one throws.
        var $read = Core\Uri::decodeComponent($bad);
        echo "accepted [", $read, "]\n";
    } catch (Throwable $notText) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
echo "tried ", $tried, " refused ", $refused, "\n";

// One of them with its message, since the refusal is this member's own and
// says which byte of the *decoded* octets is the problem rather than which
// character of the input.
try {
    echo Core\Uri::decodeComponent("a%FFb"), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $lone) {
    echo $lone->message, "\n";
}

// And the value on the accepting side of that edge, which is what makes it a
// bound rather than a blanket rule: a well-formed multi-byte sequence decodes
// to the one character it spells, and `%00` — the byte a C string could not
// hold — is an ordinary character here, so the refusal above is about UTF-8
// and nothing else. The NUL is measured rather than echoed, a raw NUL in the
// expected output being a byte no diff can show.
echo Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%C3%A9"), " ", Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%E2%82%AC"), " ", Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%F0%9F%92%A9"), "\n";
echo Core\Str::length(Core\Uri::decodeComponent("%00")), " ", Core\Str::length(Core\Uri::decodeComponent("")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
[a%] -> [a%]
[a%4] -> [a%4]
[a%zzb] -> [a%zzb]
[%41] -> [A]
[%2541] -> [%41]
tried 5 refused 5
Core\Uri::decodeComponent(): the decoded octets holds a byte a `string` cannot — byte 1 begins a sequence that is not valid UTF-8. Percent-decoding answers octets, so text carrying an escape for a non-UTF-8 byte has no `string` to decode to
é € 💩
1 0
